Troy Goode is the only person who holds at least half of the surviving lines, and only in `lib/` and `test/`. `.github/` and the root files have no majority owner.

I counted with `git blame` on the current tree, so only surviving lines count. The formatting commit (62628c0) is skipped, and `-w -M -C` ignores whitespace and moved or copied lines. I dropped dependabot's lines from the totals, about 23 in `.github/` and 2 in the root `package.json`. Troy appears under two emails (`<email redacted>` and `troy@laptop.example`), so I merged them into one person.

| Area | Top holder | Share of surviving lines | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| `lib/` | Troy Goode | 159/238 = 67% | Owner |
| `test/` | Troy Goode | 517/966 = 54% | Owner |
| `.github/` | Douglas Wilson | 118/351 = 34% | Nobody at 50% |
| root files | Troy Goode | 146/415 = 35% | Nobody at 50% |

In `.github/`, the next largest holders are Íñigo Marquínez (25%) and Sebastian Beltran (17%). In the root files, Douglas Wilson holds 28% and Jon Church 17%.

Suggested CODEOWNERS:

```
# Nobody holds >=50% of surviving lines in .github/ or the root files, so no owner is named.
/lib/   @<troy-github-handle>
/test/  @<troy-github-handle>
```

- **Handle:** I used a placeholder for Troy's GitHub handle because I only have git emails. I ran no GitHub lookups.
- **Troy's activity:** I didn't check whether he is still active. His latest commits are the two recent ones under `troy@laptop.example`, but I didn't check anything beyond that. If he has left, naming him would block reviews on every PR touching those paths.
- **Skill not used:** I did not run the busfactor skill's script. It weights commits by age rather than counting surviving lines, so it wouldn't have answered your rule.
- **Blame caveat:** Blame credits a line to whoever last changed it, so a past mechanical edit can inflate someone's share.

